lib/IO/K8s/Role/APIObject.pm -- kind() and api_version() are derived
read-only methods, but they accept and ignore an argument instead of
croaking.

Repro (measured on a hand-written class):

  IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::Pod->new->kind("Other")      --> "Pod"
  IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::Pod->new->api_version("v9")  --> "v1"

The write vanishes, no error. karr #60 removed the generated-class
*attribute* that made this look like a writable field on AutoGen classes;
what is left is this distribution-wide read-only-method behaviour, which
the #60 fix deliberately left conformant rather than diverging in one
place.

Decision needed: croak on an argument ("kind is derived from the class
name and cannot be set"), or document the swallow as intended. Croaking
is the fail-closed house line (karr #37/#39/#42) and the failure it
catches is a caller who believes they retargeted an object; against that,
it is a behaviour change on the most-called accessors in the
distribution, so it needs a Changes entry and a look at whether anything
in the wild passes an argument positionally.

Found by the autogen lane while implementing karr #60 (repro verified by
the orchestrating agent).
Lane: decision first, then io-k8s-worker.

